{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789372372,"nonce":"sdJsxnZDb4O9gsgrAEdV1rFBtus=","title":"A dynamic box is two heap allocations","message":"`zyntax_box_i64/f64/bool` allocate the `DynamicBoxRepr` and then the\npayload behind its `data` pointer: two mallocs and two frees per box.\nOn `bench_any_cast` (a million float boxes and reads), both frontends\nare slower than CPython, whose float objects come off a freelist:\n\n    any_cast   zynml 98 ms   zypy 105 ms   cpython 55 ms   pypy 71 ms\n\nand `bench_any_field` pays the same per iteration (zypy 85, zynml 51,\ncpython 151). Everything dynamic in the Python frontend goes through a\nbox, so this is a floor under every dynamic operation.\n\nOptions: a scalar payload stored inline in the box (one allocation;\n`data` points into the box itself, so every reader keeps working), a\nsize-class freelist behind the box constructors like the one behind the\nmalloc intrinsic (pool_alloc), or both. `sdk/zrtl/src/dynamic_box.rs`\nmust stay in step with the runtime's layout.","files":null}]}